pairs(correlation_data, main = "Scatterplot Matrix for Nitrate, Sulfate, and Fluoride")
```













To Develop linear regressions between single pairs of variables (Nitrate ~ Chloride),  (Fluoride ~ Sulfate) [Figures 6 & 7]: 
```{r 3rd- Linear regression, echo=FALSE}

# Create a clean data frame without missing values for both analyses
clean_data <- na.omit(Nossob[, c("NO3_mg_l", "tds_mg_l", "f_mg_l", "so4_mg_l")])

# Linear regression for Nitrate ~ TDS (Chloride)
nitrate_tds_model <- lm(NO3_mg_l ~ tds_mg_l, data = Nossob)
summary(nitrate_tds_model)

# Linear regression for Fluoride ~ Sulfate
fluoride_sulfate_model <- lm(f_mg_l ~ so4_mg_l, data = Nossob)
summary(fluoride_sulfate_model)

# Create a dataframe without missing values for plotting Nitrate vs. TDS
nitrate_tds_data <- na.omit(Nossob[, c("NO3_mg_l", "tds_mg_l")])

# Plot for Nitrate vs. TDS (Chloride)
plot(nitrate_tds_data$tds_mg_l, nitrate_tds_data$NO3_mg_l,
     xlab = "TDS (mg/L)", ylab = "Nitrate (mg/L)",
     main = "Nitrate vs. TDS (Chloride)")
abline(lm(NO3_mg_l ~ tds_mg_l, data = nitrate_tds_data), col = "blue")

# Create a clean data frame without missing values for Fluoride and SO4
fluoride_so4_data <- Nossob[!is.na(Nossob$f_mg_l) & !is.na(Nossob$so4_mg_l), 
                              c("f_mg_l", "so4_mg_l")]

# Check the dimensions of the clean data
cat("Number of rows in clean data for Fluoride and SO4:", nrow(fluoride_so4_data), "\n")

# Ensure both columns have the same length
if (nrow(fluoride_so4_data) > 0) {
    # Plot for Fluoride vs. SO4
    plot(fluoride_so4_data$so4_mg_l, fluoride_so4_data$f_mg_l,
         xlab = "Sulfate (mg/L)", ylab = "Fluoride (mg/L)",
         main = "Fluoride vs. SO4")
    abline(lm(f_mg_l ~ so4_mg_l, data = fluoride_so4_data), col = "red")
} else {
    cat("No data available for plotting Fluoride vs. SO4.\n")
}

# To calculate P-values and R-squared values: 

# For Nitrate vs. TDS model
nitrate_tds_model <- lm(NO3_mg_l ~ tds_mg_l, data = Nossob)  # Replace Nossob with your actual data frame name
nitrate_summary <- summary(nitrate_tds_model)

# Extract p-value and R-squared for Nitrate vs. TDS
nitrate_p_value <- nitrate_summary$coefficients[2, 4]  # p-value for tds_mg_l
nitrate_r_squared <- nitrate_summary$r.squared

cat("Nitrate vs. TDS: P-Value =", nitrate_p_value, " R-Squared =", nitrate_r_squared, "\n")

# For Fluoride vs. SO4 model
fluoride_sulfate_model <- lm(f_mg_l ~ so4_mg_l, data = fluoride_so4_data)  # Ensure fluoride_so4_data is defined
fluoride_summary <- summary(fluoride_sulfate_model)

# Extract p-value and R-squared for Fluoride vs. SO4
fluoride_p_value <- fluoride_summary$coefficients[2, 4]  # p-value for so4_mg_l
fluoride_r_squared <- fluoride_summary$r.squared

cat("Fluoride vs. SO4: P-Value =", fluoride_p_value, " R-Squared =", fluoride_r_squared, "\n")

``````
Is one of these linear regressions significant?
The blue line in figure 6 represents the linear relationship between Nitrate and TDS (Chloride), while the red line in figure 7 shows the relationship between Fluoride and SO4 (Sulfate). A positive slope indicates a positive relationship. 
To understand the significance of any linear regression: 
1) we have to calculate R-squared values which Indicates how well the independent variable(s) explain the variability in the dependent variable. A higher value suggests a better model fit. 
2) In addition, P-Value that Indicates the significance of the relationship between variables. A low p-value suggests that the relationship is significant. 
as the code calculated: 
Nitrate vs. TDS:        P-Value = 2.666566e-07        R-Squared = 0.213049 
Fluoride vs. SO4:      P-Value = 0.05184208           R-Squared = 0.05294456  
As we can conclude, the linear regression for Nitrate VS TDS has a bigger R-squared value and a lower P value, which indicates that this regression is significant. 







