Cluster analysis of the hydrochemistry [Figures 9 & 10]: 
```{r 5th- Cluster analysis, echo=FALSE}
# Load necessary libraries
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

# Identify hydrochemistry columns 
hydrochemistry_columns <- c("tds_mg_l", "so4_mg_l", "NO3_mg_l", "f_mg_l", "t_m2_d", "ec_ms_m", "wl_mbgl")

# Filter the dataset to include only hydrochemistry columns
Nossob_hydrochemistry <- Nossob[, hydrochemistry_columns]

# Check initial data dimensions
cat("Initial dimensions of hydrochemistry data:", dim(Nossob_hydrochemistry), "\n")

# Evaluate missingness
missing_counts <- colSums(is.na(Nossob_hydrochemistry))
print("Missing counts per column:")
print(missing_counts)

# Check dimensions after removing columns
cat("Dimensions after removing excessive missing columns:", dim(Nossob_hydrochemistry), "\n")

# Impute remaining missing values using median
for (i in seq_along(Nossob_hydrochemistry)) {
    if (is.numeric(Nossob_hydrochemistry[[i]])) {
        Nossob_hydrochemistry[[i]][is.na(Nossob_hydrochemistry[[i]])] <- median(Nossob_hydrochemistry[[i]], na.rm = TRUE)  # Replace NA with column median
    }
}

# Check for remaining non-finite values
non_finite_counts <- sapply(Nossob_hydrochemistry, function(x) sum(!is.finite(x)))
if (any(non_finite_counts > 0)) {
    cat("There are still non-finite values in the dataset.\n")
    print(non_finite_counts[non_finite_counts > 0])  # Display counts of non-finite values
} else {
    # Scale the data
    Nossob_scaled <- scale(Nossob_hydrochemistry)  # Standardize the dataset

    # Check the number of unique data points
    unique_points <- nrow(unique(Nossob_scaled))
    cat("Number of unique data points:", unique_points, "\n")

    if (unique_points > 0) {
        # Step 6: Determine the optimal number of clusters using the Elbow Method
        wss <- numeric(10)
        for (i in 1:10) {
            if (i <= unique_points) {
                wss[i] <- sum(kmeans(Nossob_scaled, centers = i, nstart = 25)$withinss)
            } else {
                wss[i] <- NA  # Assign NA if more clusters than unique points
            }
        }

        # Plot the Elbow Method
        plot(1:10, wss, type = "b", pch = 19, xlab = "Number of Clusters", ylab = "Within-cluster Sum of Squares")

        # Run K-Means with a chosen number of clusters (e.g., min(unique_points, 3))
        num_clusters <- min(unique_points, 3)  # Adjust clusters based on unique points
        set.seed(123)  # For reproducibility
        kmeans_result <- kmeans(Nossob_scaled, centers = num_clusters, nstart = 25)

        # Add cluster assignments to the original hydrochemistry data
        Nossob_hydrochemistry$Cluster <- as.factor(kmeans_result$cluster)

        # Visualize the clusters
        ggplot(Nossob_hydrochemistry, aes(x = so4_mg_l, y = NO3_mg_l, color = Cluster)) + 
            geom_point() + 
            labs(title = "Cluster Analysis of Hydrochemistry Data", x = "SO4 (mg/L)", y = "NO3 (mg/L)")
    } else {
        cat("No unique data points available for clustering.\n")
    }
}
```

Analysis: 
As we can see, we got two graphs after implementing K-Means clustering method. in which The data was standardized (z-score normalization) using the scale() function. This is important for K-Means, as it ensures that all variables contribute equally to the distance calculations. The Elbow Method was used to determine the optimal number of clusters. This involves calculating the within-cluster sum of squares (WSS) for a range of cluster counts [ the elbow graph for number of clusters]. The kmeans() function was applied to cluster the data based on the chosen number of clusters (derived from the Elbow Method). A scatter plot was created to visualize the clusters, with SO4 plotted against NO3, colored by cluster assignments. 
The Scatter Plot shows the relationship between sulfate (SO4) and nitrate (NO3) concentrations, colored by cluster assignments. You can see three distinct clusters represented in different colors. There are points that are far from the main groupings, indicating potential outliers or unique samples that might require further investigation. it indicates that the hydrochemistry data points have distinct characteristics based on the variables considered. That might mean we might have different water sources or conditions affecting the hydrochemistry of the samples.







Description of the Model: 

In this analysis, we applied several statistical techniques to study the hydrochemistry of Nossob aquifer, including correlation analysis, linear regression, multiple regression, and K-Means method for clustering analysis.
Starting with preparing a map of ground water levels helped us to understand the distribution of different points with different characteristics across Namibia and South-Africa. The analysis begins by studying the relationships between different hydrochemical variables. This helped us to understand how Fluoride, sulfate, nitrate, and total dissolved solids, are related to one another.  
We used linear regression to model the relationship between a dependent variable which is nitrate in our case and one independent variable. This allowed us to understand how changes in one hydrochemical parameter might predict changes in another. We understood how nitrate concentration could be predicted by sulfate levels. The regression coefficients provided insights into these relationships.
Moreover, multiple regression was implemented to analyze the influence of several independent variables on a single dependent variable. This approach provides more understanding of how different factors interact to affect the hydrochemical characteristics. For example, we could assess how both sulfate and total dissolved solids together influence nitrate concentrations. This model helps in understanding the effects of different hydrochemical parameters.
Finally, we applied K-Means method for clustering analysis to divide the hydrochemistry data into smaller groups based on similarities in chemical characteristics. This analysis indicated differences in water quality and potential sources of variability.
On the other hand, the correlation analysis indicated significant relationships between various hydrochemical parameters. A strong correlation between NO3 and SO4 may indicate common sources or processes affecting them both, such as agricultural runoff or industrial discharge.
The analysis indicated that the aquifer exhibits variability in chemical composition, due to factors such as:
•	Geological Influences
•	Agricultural practices, industrial discharges, and urban runoff
•	Hydrogeological Conditions such as Groundwater flow patterns, recharge areas, and interactions with surface water
•	Nutrient Levels that may suggest agricultural runoff or wastewater influences
•	Sulfate Concentrations indicates mineral weathering or contamination. 

Conclusion:
This analysis provides an understanding of the hydrochemistry of the aquifer. By integrating statistical methods to draw meaningful conclusions and insights that can inform water quality management practices and guide further research into the factors affecting the hydrochemical dynamics of the aquifer.
